course: dictionary
lesson: 13142
title: Forefoot: the front foot
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A forefoot is one of the two front feet of an animal that walks on four legs. The word fore means front, so the name tells you where the foot is. «The mole used its strong forefeet to dig a tunnel.» Forefeet is the plural, the word for more than one.
explain: Context helps you see what a forefoot does. «The horse stamped one forefoot on the cold ground.» Stamping is done with the front feet, so the clue tells you the word is about a front foot. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try "front foot" in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: The word has a second meaning in sailing. «The boat's forefoot cut through the waves.» On a ship, the forefoot is the lowest front part of the hull, where the keel meets the stem. It is the same word, but it names a part of a boat, not an animal.
fact: A forefoot is one of the front feet of an animal with four legs.
fact: Forefeet is the plural of forefoot.
fact: In science lessons, moles dig with their strong forefeet, and horses stamp with a forefoot.
fact: In sailing, a forefoot is the lowest front part of a ship's hull, where the keel meets the stem.
fact: Fore means front, so a forepaw is a front paw, just like a forefoot.
exercise: «The mole used its strong forefeet to dig a tunnel.» What are the forefeet used for here? | *Digging | Singing | Flying | Reading
exercise: What does forefoot mean? | *One of the front feet of an animal with four legs | A back leg of a bird | The back of a car | The top of a tall mast
exercise: «The boat's forefoot cut through the waves.» Where on a boat is the forefoot? | The top of the mast | *The lowest front part of the hull | The steering wheel | The back of the boat
test: Which part of a four-legged animal is a forefoot? | A tail | A horn | *A front foot | A back leg
test: In a ship, where is the forefoot? | *The lowest front part of the hull, where the keel meets the stem | The back of the deck | The sail | The top of the mast
exam: «Horses have forefeet, and a ship has a forefoot at its front.» What links the two meanings? | *Both are at the front | Both are made of wood | Both are used for digging | Both have sails
